a [snare for me, and cords; they have spread a net by the wayside; they
    have set gins for me]

                Four Ways the Wicked Seek to Ensnare
1. By snares. Hebrew: pach (HSN-6341), a spring net (Psalm 140:5; Joshua
   23:13; Job 22:10; Psalm 11:6; Psalm 69:22; Psalm 91:3; Psalm 119:110;
   Psalm 124:7; Psalm 141:9; Psalm 142:3; Proverbs 7:23; Proverbs 22:5;
   Eccles. 9:12; Isaiah 24:17-18; Jeremiah 18:22; Jeremiah 48:43-44;
   Hosea 5:1; Hosea 9:8; Amos 3:5).

2. By cords. Hebrew: chebel (HSN-2256), rope; noose (Psalm 140:5;
   Joshua 2:15; Esther 1:6; Job 36:8; Job 41:1; Proverbs 5:22; Eccles. 12:6;
   Isaiah 5:18; Jeremiah 38:6-13; Ezekiel 27:24; Hosea 11:4; Micah 2:5).

3. By nets. Hebrew: resheth (HSN-7568), a net for catching animals (Psalm
   140:5; Psalm 9:15; Psalm 10:9; Psalm 25:15; Psalm 31:4; Psalm 35:7-8;
   Psalm 57:6; Job 18:8; Proverbs 1:17; Proverbs 29:5; Lament. 1:13;
   Ezekiel 12:13; Ezekiel 17:20; Ezekiel 19:8; Ezekiel 32:3; Hosea 5:1;
   Hosea 7:12).

4. By gins. Hebrew: mowqesh (HSN-4170), snare; a noose for catching
   animals; trap (Psalm 140:5; Psalm 141:9; Amos 3:5; note, ▪ Psalm 18:5).

Snares, cords, nets, and gins refer to any means whereby the wicked seek to
take advantage of the righteous and destroy them.

